---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import HtmlMinifier from '../../components/HtmlMinifier.jsx';
---
<ToolLayout title="HTML Minifier" description="Strip whitespace and comments from HTML to reduce file size.">
  <HtmlMinifier client:load slot="tool" />

  <h2>Why minifying HTML still helps</h2>
  <p>Even with gzip/Brotli compression on most servers, removing unnecessary whitespace and comments reduces the amount of data the browser needs to parse, and can meaningfully shrink file size for content-heavy static pages.</p>

  <h2>How to use this tool</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Paste your formatted HTML.</li>
    <li>The minified version appears instantly, with size reduction shown.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Common HTML Minification Mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Minifying source files directly</strong> instead of keeping readable source and generating minified output as a build step.</li>
    <li><strong>Not verifying whitespace-sensitive elements</strong> (like <code>&lt;pre&gt;</code> tags) render correctly after minification — aggressive whitespace stripping can occasionally affect intentional formatting.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When to Use This HTML Minifier</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Quickly minifying a small HTML snippet without a full build pipeline</li>
    <li>Preparing HTML for an email template, where file size and structure matter</li>
    <li>Checking potential size savings before automating minification in a build tool</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>HTML Minifier FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>Should I minify HTML manually for a real project?</strong> For anything beyond a one-off, automate minification as part of your build process rather than doing it by hand each time.</p>
</ToolLayout>
